#: source/store/derivation/index.md:1
msgid "Store Derivation and Deriving Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:3
msgid ""
"Besides functioning as a [content-addressed store](../../glossary.md#gloss-"
"content-addressed-store), the Nix store layer works as a [build system]"
"(../../glossary.md#gloss-build-system). Other systems (like Git or IPFS) "
"also store and transfer immutable data, but they don't concern themselves "
"with _how_ that data was created."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:6
msgid ""
"This is where Nix distinguishes itself. _Derivations_ represent individual "
"build steps, and _deriving paths_ are needed to refer to the _outputs_ of "
"those build steps before they are built."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:10
msgid "Store Derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:12
msgid ""
"A derivation is a specification for running an executable on precisely "
"defined input to produce one or more [store objects](../../store/store-"
"object.md). These store objects are known as the derivation's _outputs_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:15
msgid ""
"Derivations are _built_, in which case the process is spawned according to "
"the spec, and when it exits, required to leave behind files which will "
"(after post-processing) become the outputs of the derivation. This process "
"is described in detail in [Building](../../store/building.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:23
msgid "A derivation consists of:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:25
msgid "A name"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:27
msgid ""
"An [inputs specification](#inputs), a set of [deriving paths](#deriving-path)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:29
msgid ""
"An [outputs specification](./outputs/index.md), specifying which outputs "
"should be produced, and various metadata about them."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:31
msgid ""
"The [\"system\" type](#system) (e.g. `x86_64-linux`) where the executable is "
"to run."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:33
msgid ""
"The [process creation fields](#process-creation-fields): to spawn the "
"arbitrary process which will perform the build step."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:48
msgid "Referencing derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:50
msgid ""
"Derivations are always referred to by the [store path](../../store/store-"
"path.md) of the store object they are encoded to. See the [encoding section]"
"(#derivation-encoding) for more details on how this encoding works, and thus "
"what exactly what store path we would end up with for a given derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:53
msgid ""
"The store path of the store object which encodes a derivation is often "
"called a _derivation path_ for brevity."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:55
msgid "Deriving path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:57
msgid ""
"Deriving paths are a way to refer to [store objects](../../store/store-"
"object.md) that may or may not yet be [realised](../../glossary.md#gloss-"
"realise). There are two forms:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:60
msgid ""
"\\[_constant_\\]{#deriving-path-constant}: just a [store path](../../store/"
"store-path.md). It can be made [valid](../../glossary.md#gloss-validity) by "
"copying it into the store: from the evaluator, command line interface or "
"another store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:63
msgid ""
"\\[_output_\\]{#deriving-path-output}: a pair of a [store path](../../store/"
"store-path.md) to a [store derivation](#store-derivation) and an [output](./"
"outputs/index.md) name."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:65
msgid "In pseudo code:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:82
msgid ""
"Deriving paths are necessary because, in general and particularly for "
"[content-addressing derivations](../../glossary.md#gloss-content-addressing-"
"derivation), the [store path](../../store/store-path.md) of an [output](./"
"outputs/index.md) is not known in advance. We can use an output deriving "
"path to refer to such an output, instead of the store path which we do not "
"yet know."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:88
msgid "Parts of a derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:90
msgid ""
"A derivation is constructed from the parts documented in the following "
"subsections."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:92
msgid "Inputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:94
msgid ""
"The inputs are a set of [deriving paths](#deriving-path), referring to all "
"store objects needed in order to perform this build step."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:96
msgid ""
"The [process creation fields](#process-creation-fields) will presumably "
"include many [store paths](../../store/store-path.md):"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:98
msgid "The path to the executable normally starts with a store path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:99
msgid ""
"The arguments and environment variables likely contain many other store "
"paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:101
msgid ""
"But rather than somehow scanning all the other fields for inputs, Nix "
"requires that all inputs be explicitly collected in the inputs field. It is "
"instead the responsibility of the creator of a derivation (e.g. the "
"evaluator) to ensure that every store object referenced in another field (e."
"g. referenced by store path) is included in this inputs field."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:103
msgid "System"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:105
msgid ""
"The system type on which the [`builder`](#builder) executable is meant to be "
"run."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:107
msgid ""
"A necessary condition for Nix to schedule a given derivation on some [Nix "
"instance](../../glossary.md#gloss-nix-instance) is for the \"system\" of "
"that derivation to match that instance's [`system` configuration option]"
"(../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-system) or [`extra-platforms` "
"configuration option](../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-extra-platforms)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:109
msgid ""
"By putting the `system` in each derivation, Nix allows _heterogeneous_ build "
"plans, where not all steps can be run on the same machine or same sort of "
"machine. Nix can schedule builds such that it automatically builds on other "
"platforms by [forwarding build requests](../../advanced-topics/distributed-"
"builds.md) to other Nix instances."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:120
msgid "Process creation fields"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:122
msgid ""
"These are the three fields which describe how to spawn the process which "
"(along with any of its own child processes) will perform the build. You may "
"note that this has everything needed for an `execve` system call."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:125
msgid "Builder"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:127
msgid ""
"This is the path to an executable that will perform the build and produce "
"the [outputs](./outputs/index.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:129
msgid "Arguments"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:131
msgid ""
"Command-line arguments to be passed to the [`builder`](#builder) executable."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:133
msgid ""
"Note that these are the arguments after the first argument. The first "
"argument passed to the `builder` will be the value of `builder`, as per the "
"usual convention on Unix. See [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Argv) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:137
msgid "Environment Variables"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:139
msgid ""
"Environment variables which will be passed to the [builder](#builder) "
"executable."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:141
msgid ""
"Each environment variable additionally has a \\[_pass as file_\\]{#pass-as-"
"file} flag. When it is set, rather than passing the value directly in the "
"environment, the value is written to a temporary file in the build "
"directory, and an environment variable named like the original but with a "
"`Path` suffix is set to the path of that file. For example, a variable `big` "
"with _pass as file_ set results in an environment variable `bigPath` "
"pointing to a file containing the value of `big`. This is useful for large "
"values, since most operating systems impose a limit on the size of the "
"process environment (typically a few hundred kilobytes)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:146
msgid ""
"This flag has no effect when [structured attributes](#structured-attrs) are "
"used, as attributes are then not passed through the environment in the first "
"place, so there is no size constraint to avoid."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:148
msgid "Structured Attributes"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:150
msgid "Nix also has special support for embedding JSON in the derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:152
msgid ""
"Structured attributes obviate the need for the [_pass as file_](#pass-as-"
"file) flag, since JSON files have no size restrictions, unlike process "
"environments. They also allow [options](#options) and [output checks]"
"(outputs/index.md#output-checks) to be expressed in a structured way, rather "
"than string-encoded in environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:155
msgid ""
"The environment variable `NIX_ATTRS_JSON_FILE` points to the exact location "
"of that file both in a build and a [`nix-shell`](../../command-ref/nix-shell."
"md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:157
msgid ""
"As a convenience to Bash builders, Nix writes a script that initialises "
"shell variables corresponding to all attributes that are representable in "
"Bash. The environment variable `NIX_ATTRS_SH_FILE` points to the exact "
"location of the script, both in a build and a [`nix-shell`](../../command-"
"ref/nix-shell.md). This includes non-nested (associative) arrays. For "
"example, the attribute `hardening.format = true` ends up as the Bash "
"associative array element `${hardening[format]}`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:162
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:164
msgid ""
"Derivations carry a number of _options_ influencing how they are built and "
"scheduled. Historically, these were encoded with specially-named "
"[environment variables](#env) or [structured attributes](#structured-attrs); "
"they are, however, fields of the derivation in their own right."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:167
msgid ""
"Options that apply to individual outputs — [output checks](outputs/index."
"md#output-checks) and [reference scanning](outputs/index.md#reference-"
"scanning) — are documented with [derivation outputs](outputs/index.md). The "
"remaining options apply to the derivation as a whole:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:170
msgid ""
"\\[_export references graph_\\]{#export-references-graph}: A map from file "
"names to sets of [deriving paths](#deriving-path). For each entry, the "
"_references graph_ of the given store objects — each store object in their "
"[closure](../../glossary.md#gloss-closure), along with its references — is "
"made available to the builder: as a file of the given name in the build "
"directory (in the textual format understood by [`nix-store --register-"
"validity`](../../command-ref/nix-store/register-validity.md), with the "
"deriver fields left empty and no hashes), or, with [structured attributes]"
"(#structured-attrs), under the given key in the JSON attributes file (as a "
"list of store object infos)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:178
msgid "This lets a builder do something with the closure of its inputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:180
msgid "**Usage note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:182
msgid ""
"Examples of builders using this include the builders in NixOS that generate "
"the initial ramdisk for booting Linux (a `cpio` archive containing the "
"closure of the boot script) and the ISO-9660 image for the installation CD "
"(which is populated with a Nix store containing the closure of a bootable "
"NixOS configuration)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:185
msgid ""
"\\[_required system features_\\]{#required-system-features}: A set of "
"features that Nix must have among its [`system-features`](../../command-ref/"
"conf-file.md#conf-system-features) for the derivation to be scheduled on it, "
"in addition to the [system](#system) type matching."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:188
msgid ""
"\\[_prefer local build_\\]{#prefer-local-build}: A hint that the derivation "
"is cheapest to build locally: when [distributed building is enabled](../../"
"command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-builders), it will, if possible, be built "
"locally rather than forwarded to a remote machine."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:191
msgid ""
"\\[_allow substitutes_\\]{#allow-substitutes}: Whether the derivation's "
"outputs may be [substituted](../../glossary.md#gloss-substitute). When "
"disabled, the derivation is always built (locally or remotely); this is "
"useful for derivations that are cheaper to build than to substitute. Nix may "
"ignore this option via the [`always-allow-substitutes`](../../command-ref/"
"conf-file.md#conf-always-allow-substitutes) configuration option."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:196 source/store/derivation/index.md:237
#: source/store/derivation/index.md:280 source/store/derivation/index.md:337
msgid "**Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:198
msgid ""
"When disabled, some Nix instance must be able to run the [builder](#builder) "
"on the specified [system](#system) type, since the derivation cannot be "
"substituted."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:200
msgid ""
"\\[_impure environment variables_\\]{#impure-env-vars}: A set of environment "
"variables passed from the environment of the calling user to the builder, "
"which otherwise starts from a cleared environment. This is only allowed for "
"[fixed-output derivations](../../glossary.md#gloss-fixed-output-derivation), "
"where such impurities are tolerable because (the hash of) the output is "
"known in advance. It is ignored for all other derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:205
msgid "**Warning**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:207
msgid ""
"The variables are taken from the environment of the building process itself: "
"when building via a daemon, from the daemon's environment, and only "
"otherwise from the environment of the calling command, e.g. `nix-build`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:210
msgid ""
"If the [`configurable-impure-env` experimental feature](../../development/"
"experimental-features.md#xp-feature-configurable-impure-env) is enabled, "
"these environment variables can also be controlled through the [`impure-env`]"
"(../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-impure-env) configuration option."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:213
msgid "Sandbox-related options:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:215
msgid ""
"\\[_no chroot_\\]{#no-chroot}: The derivation asks to be built without "
"[sandboxing](../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-sandbox). Only under "
"certain configurations will this wish actually be respected, however. Nix "
"may instead simply fail the build, rather than try to perform it without "
"sandboxing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:220
msgid ""
"\\[_additional sandbox profile_\\]{#additional-sandbox-profile}: (Darwin "
"only) Additional sandbox profile text, granting the build extra permissions "
"inside the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:223
msgid ""
"\\[_impure host dependencies_\\]{#impure-host-deps}: (Darwin only) A set of "
"host system paths made available inside the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:226
msgid ""
"\\[_allow local networking_\\]{#allow-local-networking}: (Darwin only) "
"Whether the build may access the local network inside the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:232
msgid "Placeholders"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:234
msgid ""
"Placeholders are opaque values used within the [process creation fields]"
"(#process-creation-fields) to \\[store objects\\] for which we don't yet "
"know [store path](../../store/store-path.md)s. They are strings in the form "
"`/<hash>` that are embedded anywhere within the strings of those fields, and "
"we are [considering](https://github.com/NixOS/nix/issues/12361) to add store-"
"path-like placeholders."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:239
msgid ""
"Output Deriving Path exist to solve the same problem as placeholders --- "
"that is, referring to store objects for which we don't yet know a store "
"path. They also have a string syntax with `^`, [described in the encoding "
"section](#deriving-path-encoding). We could use that syntax instead of `/"
"<hash>` for placeholders, but its human-legibility would cause problems."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:243
msgid ""
"There are two types of placeholder, corresponding to the two cases where "
"this problem arises:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:245
msgid "\\[Output placeholder\\]{#output-placeholder}:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:247
msgid "This is a placeholder for a derivation's own output."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:249
msgid "\\[Input placeholder\\]{#input-placeholder}:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:251
msgid ""
"This is a placeholder to a derivation's non-constant [input](#inputs), i.e. "
"an input that is an \\[output derived path\\]."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:254
msgid "**Explanation**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:256
msgid ""
"In general, we need to [realise](../../glossary.md#gloss-realise) a [store "
"object](../../store/store-object.md) in order to be sure to have a store "
"object for it. But for these two cases this is either impossible or "
"impractical:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:259
msgid "In the output case this is impossible:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:261
msgid ""
"We cannot build the output until we have a correct derivation, and we cannot "
"have a correct derivation (without using placeholders) until we have the "
"output path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:263
msgid "In the input case this is impractical:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:265
msgid ""
"If we always build a dependency first, and then refer to its output by store "
"path, we would lose the ability for a derivation graph to describe an entire "
"build plan consisting of multiple build steps."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:267 source/store/derivation/index.md:376
msgid "Encoding"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:269
msgid "Derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:271
msgid "There are two formats, documented separately:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:273
msgid "The legacy [\"ATerm\" format](../../protocols/derivation-aterm.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:275
msgid ""
"The experimental, currently under development and changing [JSON format]"
"(../../protocols/json/derivation/index.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:277
msgid ""
"Every derivation has a canonical choice of encoding used to serialize it to "
"a store object. This ensures that there is a canonical [store path](../../"
"store/store-path.md) used to refer to the derivation, as described in "
"[Referencing derivations](#derivation-path)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:282
msgid ""
"Currently, the canonical encoding for every derivation is the \"ATerm\" "
"format, but this is subject to change for the types of derivations which are "
"not yet stable."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:285
msgid ""
"Regardless of the format used, when serializing a derivation to a store "
"object, that store object will be content-addressed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:287
msgid "In the common case, the inputs to store objects are either:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:289
msgid ""
"[constant deriving paths](#deriving-path-constant) for content-addressed "
"source objects, which are \"initial inputs\" rather than the outputs of some "
"other derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:291
msgid "the outputs of other derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:293
msgid ""
"If those other derivations _also_ abide by this common case (and likewise "
"for transitive inputs), then the entire closure of the serialized derivation "
"will be content-addressed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:295
msgid "Deriving Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:297
msgid "_constant_"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:299
msgid ""
"Constant deriving paths are encoded simply as the underlying store path is. "
"Thus, we see that every encoded store path is also a valid encoded "
"(constant) deriving path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:302
msgid "_output_"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:304
msgid "Output deriving paths are encoded by"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:306
msgid "encoding of a store path referring to a derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:308
msgid "a `^` separator (or `!` in some legacy contexts)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:310
msgid "the name of an output of the previously referred derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:312 source/store/derivation/index.md:381
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:318
msgid "This parses like so:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:320
msgid ""
"```\n"
"/nix/store/lxrn8v5aamkikg6agxwdqd1jz7746wz4-firefox-98.0.2.drv^out\n"
"|------------------------------------------------------------| |-|\n"
"store path (usual encoding)                                    output name\n"
"                                                          |--|\n"
"                                                          note the \".drv\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:328
msgid "Extending the model to be higher-order"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:330
msgid ""
"**Experimental feature**: [`dynamic-derivations`](../../development/"
"experimental-features.md#xp-feature-dynamic-derivations)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:332
msgid ""
"So far, we have used store paths to refer to derivations. That works because "
"we've implicitly assumed that all derivations are created _statically_ --- "
"created by some mechanism out of band, and then manually inserted into the "
"store. But what if derivations could also be created dynamically within Nix? "
"In other words, what if derivations could be the outputs of other "
"derivations?"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:339
msgid ""
"In the parlance of \"Build Systems à la carte\", we are generalizing the Nix "
"store layer to be a \"Monadic\" instead of \"Applicative\" build system."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:341
msgid ""
"How should we refer to such derivations? A deriving path works, the same as "
"how we refer to other derivation outputs. But what about a dynamic "
"derivations output? (i.e. how do we refer to the output of a derivation, "
"which is itself an output of a derivation?) For that we need to generalize "
"the definition of deriving path, replacing the store path used to refer to "
"the derivation with a nested deriving path:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:355
msgid ""
"Now, the `drvPath` field of `OutputPath` is itself a `DerivingPath` instead "
"of a `StorePath`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:357
msgid "With that change, here is updated definition:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:374
msgid ""
"Under this extended model, `DerivingPath`s are thus inductively built up "
"from a root `ConstantPath`, wrapped with zero or more outer `OutputPath`s."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:378
msgid ""
"The encoding is adjusted in the natural way, encoding the `drv` field "
"recursively using the same deriving path encoding. The result of this is "
"that it is possible to have a chain of `^<output-name>` at the end of the "
"final string, as opposed to just a single one."
msgstr ""
